1
00:00:00,417 --> 00:00:02,737
One sentence, from the brief.

2
00:00:02,777 --> 00:00:06,210
Graduates working in finance, in London, for more than three years.

3
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:08,710
One sentence. Four answers.

4
00:00:08,750 --> 00:00:13,293
The file: 525 records, for 500 members.

5
00:00:13,333 --> 00:00:16,627
Under the loupe, London is spelled 8 different ways.

6
00:00:16,667 --> 00:00:19,463
And 25 people appear twice.

7
00:00:19,583 --> 00:00:22,864
Filter the file as delivered: 2 members.

8
00:00:22,917 --> 00:00:25,793
Normalise the spellings: 26.

9
00:00:25,833 --> 00:00:30,729
Remove the duplicates, read employers and job titles: 49.

10
00:00:32,083 --> 00:00:34,127
But look at the dates.

11
00:00:34,167 --> 00:00:38,710
68 % of profiles have not been touched in 2 years or more.

12
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:43,644
“Three years in post” and “three years without an update” are the same signal.

13
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:47,950
One user put it plainly: “340 London expats.

14
00:00:47,990 --> 00:00:51,879
Twenty had moved back years ago. Two unsubscribed.”

15
00:00:53,333 --> 00:00:55,593
So the 49 split by trust.

16
00:00:55,633 --> 00:01:00,043
1 current. 5 to confirm. 43 to reactivate.

17
00:01:00,417 --> 00:01:02,831
6 can be e-mailed today.

18
00:01:04,167 --> 00:01:06,847
Describe your audience in one sentence.

19
00:01:06,887 --> 00:01:08,847
Get a segment you can trust.

20
00:01:08,887 --> 00:01:11,627
No member record ever leaves the platform.

21
00:01:11,667 --> 00:01:13,867
Trusted Segments.
